// ============================================================================
// DAILY — 2026-10-21 (Wednesday): Today's date as pattern
// ============================================================================
// Weekly rotation slot: the calendar date itself drives the pattern. Today
// (IST) is 2026-10-21 -- day 21, month 10 (October), year 2026.
//
// THE DERIVATION (this is the whole point of the day):
//   Recent Wednesdays each found a different relationship between day and
//   month: a digit sequence (08-26), three clock rates (09-02), a hocket
//   when day==month (09-09), two independent coprime grid-lengths (09-16),
//   per-token Euclidean flutters (09-23), a GCD fraction-reduction into a
//   10-against-3 polyrhythm (09-30), day/month fed straight into
//   `.euclid(7,10)` as a rhythmic mask (10-07), and day/month's binary bits
//   compared bit-by-bit (10-14). Today day (21) and month (10) are AGAIN
//   coprime (GCD(21,10)=1, the same relationship as 09-16/10-07) -- but
//   rather than reaching for another grid-pair or another `.euclid()` mask
//   (both already used on exactly this coprime relationship), this time the
//   actual STEPS of the Euclidean algorithm that PROVES the GCD become the
//   structure, not just its answer:
//     21 = 2 x 10 + 1   (divide day by month: quotient 2, remainder 1)
//     10 = 10 x 1 + 0   (divide month by the remainder: quotient 10, exact)
//   GCD = 1 confirms the coprimality, but the quotients (2, 10) and the
//   remainder (1) are what drive the music: day's number, measured in
//   month-sized units, is exactly TWO complete tens plus ONE left over
//   (21 = 10 + 10 + 1). That three-part split -- not the habitual
//   call/response/tension/resolution four-phrase template every prior
//   Wednesday has used -- becomes the form: two full "month-length" (10)
//   phrases, then one lone "remainder" phrase, cycling in a 3-slot `<...>`
//   instead of 4. The day's raw value (21) is never used directly as a
//   scale degree -- at 21 mod 7 = 0 it would overshoot three full octaves
//   above tonic, an ugly, untasteful leap for a short line -- so the
//   piece uses the algorithm's own derived numbers (2, 10, 1) instead,
//   the same taste call MUSIC-QC-LOOP.md asks for over literal arithmetic.
//
//   Two structural tones anchor every phrase, the project's established
//   day/month-mod-7 mapping (honest reuse, unchanged mapping): day 21
//   mod 7 = 0 -> tonic anchors DAY's phrases. Month 10 mod 7 = 3 -> scale
//   degree 3 anchors MONTH's phrases, the same month value (10) as 10-07,
//   reaching the same overshoot peak (degree 10, one octave + degree 3)
//   for the exact same honest reason -- the month genuinely hasn't changed.
//
//   DAY (tonic-anchored, mask "<[10 tokens] [10 tokens] 0>"): PHRASE ONE
//   (CALL) rises in leaps through a wide arc and ends open on the octave;
//   PHRASE TWO (RESPONSE) is CALL's near-exact retrograde, answering back
//   down to a doubled tonic landing -- a real retrograde device, reversed
//   pitch-for-pitch; PHRASE THREE is the remainder itself made audible: a
//   single held tonic note for the entire cycle, long attack/release, the
//   one moment with no motion at all -- "1" has nothing left to subdivide.
//
//   MONTH (degree-3-anchored, same 3-slot form, deliberately sparser):
//   PHRASE ONE and TWO punctuate degree 3 and the degree-10 overshoot peak
//   with rests between, a true countermelody to DAY's denser lines; PHRASE
//   THREE sits silent through nearly the whole remainder cycle except one
//   late pickup note right at its very end -- a one-note bridge back into
//   the loop's restart, MONTH's own answer to DAY's single sustained note.
//
//   CYCLES = 23 = day-digit-sum (2+1=3) + month (10) + year-digit-sum
//   (2+0+2+6=10). setcpm(60) x 23 cycles / 60 = 23s.
//
// Scale: B minor -- fresh for this slot (last three Wednesdays used E
// minor, G minor, and C minor); the algorithm is the idea, not the mode.
//
// NO DRUM KIT, deliberately, not by default: the 2-tens-plus-a-remainder
// form IS the piece's audible structure, read straight off the actual
// Euclidean-algorithm steps for today's date -- a metric kit underneath
// would just restate a pulse that's already there, the reflexive-
// percussion failure MUSIC-QC-LOOP.md flags.
//
// Instrumentation, chosen for what's distinctive about THIS week's device
// (a quotient/remainder split, not a bit-mask or a ratio), and a fresh
// pairing from the last three Wednesdays (saw+triangle, supersaw+sine,
// square+triangle): DAY gets `square` -- clean and whole, fitting the
// "exact division" half of the idea (MUSIC-KNOWLEDGE.md §1's digital row).
// MONTH gets `sawtooth` -- a busier-grained edge for the finer, sparser
// countermelody voice. DRONE stays `sine` -- tanpura convention, tonic and
// fifth, no automation (§2.5, §3). Only DAY carries the acid taste layer
// (light distortion + a filter sweep); the sweep period (2 cycles) is the
// OTHER derived number -- the quotient of complete tens (2) -- so the
// piece's one automated parameter ties back to the same algorithm as the
// form, not a number pulled in from a different week's formula.
//
// This is a DAILY quick clip, not a flagship set -- one continuous pattern,
// no alap/jor/jhala arc.
// ============================================================================

setcpm(60)
let CYCLES = 23 // day-digit-sum (3) + month (10) + year-digit-sum (10)

stack(
  // DAY -- tonic-anchored (21 mod 7 = 0). Three-slot form reading 21 as
  // 2 complete month-lengths (10) plus a remainder (1), not the habitual
  // four-phrase call/response/tension/resolution template: PHRASE ONE
  // (CALL) rises in leaps to the month-overshoot peak and ends open;
  // PHRASE TWO (RESPONSE) is CALL's near-exact retrograde, landing on a
  // doubled tonic; PHRASE THREE is the remainder itself -- one held note,
  // nothing left to subdivide.
  n("<[0 ~ 3 5 7 5 10 7 ~ 7] [7 ~ 10 7 5 7 ~ 3 0 0] 0>")
    .scale("B:minor")
    .sound("square")
    .attack(.006).decay(.11).sustain(0).release(.05)
    .distort(.15) // acid taste layer, kept light so the retrograde stays legible
    .lpf(sine.range(500, 2400).slow(2)) // sweep period = the quotient of complete tens (2)
    .gain(.32)
    .pan(.68)
    .room(1.1),

  // MONTH -- degree-3-anchored (10 mod 7 = 3), reaching the same degree-10
  // overshoot peak as 10-07 for the same honest reason (month is still
  // 10). Sparser countermelody, same 3-slot form: PHRASE ONE and TWO
  // punctuate degree 3 and the peak with rests between; PHRASE THREE sits
  // silent through the remainder cycle except one late pickup note right
  // at the very end -- a one-note bridge back into the loop.
  n("<[3 ~ ~ 6 ~ 3 ~ 10 ~ ~] [~ 10 ~ 3 ~ 6 ~ ~ 3 ~] [~ ~ ~ ~ ~ ~ ~ ~ ~ 6]>")
    .scale("B:minor")
    .sound("sawtooth")
    .attack(.04).decay(.3).sustain(.15).release(.9)
    .gain(.27)
    .pan(.32)
    .room(2.6),

  // DRONE -- tanpura convention, tonic/fifth, held for the full piece with
  // no automation (the one moving filter this week lives on DAY instead).
  note("[b2,f#2]/23")
    .sound("sine")
    .attack(1.4).release(2)
    .gain(.22)
    .pan(.5)
    .room(3.2)
)
